cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this software or its results, please cite it as below."
title: "The lonely runner conjecture for eight and nine runners: a kernel-checked proof"
abstract: >-
  A formalization in Lean 4 with Mathlib of the lonely runner conjecture for eight and nine runners:
  1/8 <= ML(v) for every seven and 1/9 <= ML(v) for every eight positive integer speeds. The two theorems have no
  hypothesis and depend only on propext, Classical.choice and Quot.sound (no sorry, no native_decide). The proof
  uses a formalization of the seven-runner case of Barajas and Serra, reductions to finite certificates for 27
  primes (eight runners) and 36 primes (nine runners), generated by Rust programs and checked by the Lean kernel,
  and a bridge from the eight-runner to the nine-runner theorem. Both cases are known from computer-assisted
  proofs in the literature; no new case of the conjecture is claimed. The archive contains the Lean modules
  (18,139 shipped of a 25,988-module import closure; the omitted generated data modules and the generated
  eight-runner sources are listed with sha256 and regenerated by scripts), the Rust generators and a
  reproduction entry point. The final assembly was elaborated once with raw lean over restored and staged
  libraries that were not recompiled in that run; no from-scratch rebuild in one run was made. The Lean modules,
  the Rust generators and the first draft of the paper were produced by an AI system (Claude, Anthropic); the
  questions, directions and the decision to formalize these statements came from Vico Bonfioli. The paper has not
  been peer reviewed.
authors:
  - family-names: Bonfioli
    given-names: Vico
